        "math_academy_id": "q-106479",
        "problem": "Find all of the solutions to ${x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}-8x+40=0$ given that $x=1+3\\text{i}$ is a solution.",
        "worked_solution": "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}-8x+40.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=1+3\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{\u2015}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{\u2015}{1+3\\text{i}}=1-3\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}-(1+3\\text{i})x-(1-3\\text{i})x+(1+3\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+({1}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-2x+10$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+4 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-2x+10\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}-8x+40\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}}_{\u2015} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}-8x+40 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}-8x+40}_{\u2015} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-2x+10)({x}^{2}+4).$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+4=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots of our quartic equation are $x=1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,\\pm 2\\text{i}.$",
